WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.830 --> 00:00:08.130
&gt;&gt; Today, I'm pleased
to have David.

00:00:08.131 --> 00:00:12.270
David Lomet accepted to do
this talk on cost-performance.

00:00:12.271 --> 00:00:13.860
He gave a really
interesting talk at

00:00:13.861 --> 00:00:17.130
the Daimon workshop, at Sigmod,

00:00:17.131 --> 00:00:19.800
and was under a lot of
time pressure there

00:00:19.801 --> 00:00:22.490
for obviously for
shorter presentation,

00:00:22.491 --> 00:00:24.410
so he'll have
a little more time today

00:00:24.411 --> 00:00:28.650
to really spell out
the details. So, Dave.

00:00:28.870 --> 00:00:31.160
&gt;&gt; Thanks Phil. So, this

00:00:31.161 --> 00:00:35.215
is my effort to give
an entertaining talk,

00:00:35.216 --> 00:00:37.290
as well as an informative one.

00:00:37.291 --> 00:00:39.475
We'll have to see how that goes.

00:00:39.476 --> 00:00:41.810
So, this is pretty
much the same slides

00:00:41.811 --> 00:00:43.920
that I used at Daimon.

00:00:43.921 --> 00:00:48.030
So, what's happening in
traditional databases

00:00:48.031 --> 00:00:53.250
and what's the situation
that we're facing?

00:00:53.251 --> 00:00:56.280
So, this is a slide
that I unscrupulously

00:00:56.281 --> 00:00:59.600
borrowed the idea from
Mike Stonebraker,

00:00:59.601 --> 00:01:03.530
where he showed that the
traditional database systems are

00:01:03.531 --> 00:01:07.990
being squeezed by
the special purpose datastores.

00:01:07.991 --> 00:01:11.100
So, this is the way
the picture looks,

00:01:11.101 --> 00:01:12.430
you got streaming on one corner,

00:01:12.431 --> 00:01:14.000
column store on the other end and

00:01:14.001 --> 00:01:16.185
main memory row stores
at the top,

00:01:16.186 --> 00:01:19.600
and the situation
is perceived to be

00:01:19.601 --> 00:01:23.185
getting even worse and these
separate areas are growing,

00:01:23.186 --> 00:01:26.340
and the comment that
Stonebraker made

00:01:26.341 --> 00:01:30.015
was instead of having
a one size fits all,

00:01:30.016 --> 00:01:31.640
we have a one size fits none,

00:01:31.641 --> 00:01:33.830
where the traditional
vendors are really

00:01:33.831 --> 00:01:36.680
being squeezed and their markets
are being taken away,

00:01:36.681 --> 00:01:41.435
and so this is a pretty
challenging situation.

00:01:41.436 --> 00:01:47.735
So, the question is are
the traditional. Yes.

00:01:47.736 --> 00:01:49.010
&gt;&gt; The previous slide
[inaudible].

00:01:49.011 --> 00:02:04.395
&gt;&gt; In a few slides
you'll have an answer.

00:02:04.396 --> 00:02:06.450
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:02:06.451 --> 00:02:09.030
&gt;&gt; So yeah. Main memory system of

00:02:09.031 --> 00:02:11.770
the traditional vendors
maybe are dead,

00:02:11.771 --> 00:02:13.580
and what about Amazon Aurora?

00:02:13.581 --> 00:02:17.110
Well, they may be next.

00:02:19.460 --> 00:02:22.360
This is not what's happening.

00:02:22.361 --> 00:02:26.270
In fact, the elephants
are continuing to dance,

00:02:26.271 --> 00:02:28.025
there's been no sign that

00:02:28.026 --> 00:02:32.030
the streaming folks or

00:02:32.031 --> 00:02:35.760
the column-store folks or
the main memory database folks,

00:02:35.761 --> 00:02:37.580
are really taking
significant market share

00:02:37.581 --> 00:02:39.290
from the traditional vendors,

00:02:39.291 --> 00:02:41.480
and so the question is why?

00:02:41.481 --> 00:02:44.280
So, here's what's happening,

00:02:44.281 --> 00:02:48.655
you got the traditional
vendors, with streaming,

00:02:48.656 --> 00:02:51.005
they've formed
a partnership where

00:02:51.006 --> 00:02:54.095
streaming and traditional
database vendors

00:02:54.096 --> 00:02:55.155
live side-by-side,

00:02:55.156 --> 00:02:58.685
happily in peace, walking
off into the sunset.

00:02:58.686 --> 00:03:01.849
The column store stuff has
been pretty much absorbed

00:03:01.850 --> 00:03:05.120
into the main traditional
database engines,

00:03:05.121 --> 00:03:08.185
and the main memory row stores,

00:03:08.186 --> 00:03:10.270
well, they're sort of
a niche market, right?

00:03:10.271 --> 00:03:12.330
So, the question is,

00:03:12.331 --> 00:03:14.880
and what I'm going to
focus mostly on is

00:03:14.881 --> 00:03:18.065
main memory row store part
and the niche market part,

00:03:18.066 --> 00:03:20.200
and the question to
be asked is why,

00:03:20.201 --> 00:03:22.120
why is that happening, why

00:03:22.121 --> 00:03:25.205
has that turned out
to be the result?

00:03:25.206 --> 00:03:28.365
So, there's economic
factors in play

00:03:28.366 --> 00:03:33.060
and so we're working on
cloud infrastructure.

00:03:33.061 --> 00:03:36.705
The cloud infrastructure
has to be cheap commodity.

00:03:36.706 --> 00:03:39.900
The software infrastructure
has to run effectively

00:03:39.901 --> 00:03:43.515
on that commodity hardware.

00:03:43.516 --> 00:03:46.615
This is a world where there's
elastic provisioning.

00:03:46.616 --> 00:03:49.090
That is to say, when
somebody doesn't use

00:03:49.091 --> 00:03:50.890
the resources you
want to free them up

00:03:50.891 --> 00:03:53.285
so that someone else
can use them instead.

00:03:53.286 --> 00:03:56.490
You want basically
the resources to

00:03:56.491 --> 00:03:57.520
go to the person who's got

00:03:57.521 --> 00:03:59.540
the most money and
willing to pay for them,

00:03:59.541 --> 00:04:02.200
and so you want to
give up resources

00:04:02.201 --> 00:04:06.430
to higher valued applications.

00:04:06.431 --> 00:04:08.620
So, the fact is that money talks,

00:04:08.621 --> 00:04:10.835
it doesn't matter whether
what kind of money it is,

00:04:10.836 --> 00:04:13.465
money talks, matters a lot.

00:04:13.466 --> 00:04:16.210
So, what we'll see is that money

00:04:16.211 --> 00:04:18.790
is talking very clearly
I think in this case,

00:04:18.791 --> 00:04:24.175
and so let's proceed
and see what happens.

00:04:24.176 --> 00:04:28.375
So, what's the story with
traditional database systems?

00:04:28.376 --> 00:04:31.985
They're what I call
caching data stores.

00:04:31.986 --> 00:04:33.410
They are not main memory stores,

00:04:33.411 --> 00:04:35.120
they are caching data stores.

00:04:35.121 --> 00:04:37.660
So, traditional database
systems actually

00:04:37.661 --> 00:04:40.910
do offer good cost-performance.

00:04:41.550 --> 00:04:44.080
The costs vary.

00:04:44.081 --> 00:04:48.630
So, we need to stop being
fixated solely on performance,

00:04:48.631 --> 00:04:50.065
we need to start thinking about

00:04:50.066 --> 00:04:52.699
cost as well as performance.

00:04:53.700 --> 00:04:57.215
Traditional database systems have

00:04:57.216 --> 00:05:01.270
performance that is lower
than the main memory systems,

00:05:01.271 --> 00:05:03.790
that's a fact of life,

00:05:03.791 --> 00:05:05.575
and yet they succeed in having

00:05:05.576 --> 00:05:08.515
better cost performance,
which is interesting.

00:05:08.516 --> 00:05:12.490
Why should that be? So, what

00:05:12.491 --> 00:05:13.840
I'm going to do is
suggest also at

00:05:13.841 --> 00:05:17.360
the end a change in
the focus of our field to

00:05:17.361 --> 00:05:19.490
not surprisingly be more focused

00:05:19.491 --> 00:05:22.385
on cost performance
than pure performance.

00:05:22.386 --> 00:05:25.250
So, caching, what is
caching data store?

00:05:25.251 --> 00:05:26.760
Well, it uses a cache,

00:05:26.761 --> 00:05:29.360
of course it does,

00:05:29.361 --> 00:05:31.980
right? What is the cache?

00:05:31.981 --> 00:05:33.200
While you move data from

00:05:33.201 --> 00:05:34.990
secondary storage to main memory,

00:05:34.991 --> 00:05:36.950
when the data needs
to be operated

00:05:36.951 --> 00:05:39.190
on and you move it back
out of main memory,

00:05:39.191 --> 00:05:41.570
when you're through using it
or when it's no longer makes

00:05:41.571 --> 00:05:45.180
any sense to keep it in cache
because it's no longer hot.

00:05:46.360 --> 00:05:49.790
So, it's this movement between

00:05:49.791 --> 00:05:52.880
main memory and secondary
storage which is

00:05:52.881 --> 00:05:55.105
the significant factor in why

00:05:55.106 --> 00:05:57.170
the traditional database systems

00:05:57.171 --> 00:05:59.810
are in fact still
succeeding very well.

00:05:59.811 --> 00:06:02.135
The fact is, here the data lives

00:06:02.136 --> 00:06:04.940
permanently on secondary
storage and it's only brought

00:06:04.941 --> 00:06:07.250
into the main memory as

00:06:07.251 --> 00:06:11.070
a cache improvisation where
you act on it in main memory,

00:06:11.071 --> 00:06:13.990
and that's a very
significant factor.

00:06:13.991 --> 00:06:17.790
So, I'm going to talk about
two forms of operation.

00:06:17.791 --> 00:06:20.080
This is a little bit

00:06:20.130 --> 00:06:24.145
of an abstract way of
thinking about it,

00:06:24.146 --> 00:06:26.150
but here's what I'm going to look

00:06:26.151 --> 00:06:28.910
at main memory operations
that we call MM,

00:06:28.911 --> 00:06:32.635
they're operations which find
the data in main memory,

00:06:32.636 --> 00:06:34.205
and they're very fast.

00:06:34.206 --> 00:06:35.650
You find the data in main memory,

00:06:35.651 --> 00:06:39.170
you just have
execution path through

00:06:39.171 --> 00:06:41.240
your access method or whatever

00:06:41.241 --> 00:06:45.370
and get the shovel the results
back out to our users.

00:06:45.430 --> 00:06:48.020
The second set of

00:06:48.021 --> 00:06:51.575
operations is what I call
secondary storage operations.

00:06:51.576 --> 00:06:54.050
Secondary storage operations
are the ones where you

00:06:54.051 --> 00:06:57.890
find the data on
secondary storage,

00:06:58.220 --> 00:07:01.465
usually that means
flash nowadays,

00:07:01.466 --> 00:07:05.190
and they're more
expensive to execute by

00:07:05.191 --> 00:07:07.205
a not insignificant factor

00:07:07.206 --> 00:07:08.970
than the main memory
operations are.

00:07:08.971 --> 00:07:11.960
The reason why that is is
because we are dealing

00:07:11.961 --> 00:07:15.565
with data that's sitting
currently on secondary storage,

00:07:15.566 --> 00:07:18.075
you have to bring it
into memory first,

00:07:18.076 --> 00:07:19.845
that's an operation which

00:07:19.846 --> 00:07:24.395
costs some amount of
dollars and time,

00:07:24.396 --> 00:07:26.870
and then you have to do
to main memory operation

00:07:26.871 --> 00:07:29.990
after you bring the operation
into main memory.

00:07:29.991 --> 00:07:33.680
So, that's going to be
more expensive both in

00:07:33.681 --> 00:07:38.245
terms of performance impact
and in terms of dollars.

00:07:38.246 --> 00:07:41.295
So, looking at it this way,

00:07:41.296 --> 00:07:43.820
caching stores look like
a bad idea and here's

00:07:43.821 --> 00:07:47.090
a chart which shows
why it's a bad idea.

00:07:47.091 --> 00:07:50.540
What it shows is that as you mix

00:07:50.541 --> 00:07:52.850
secondary storage
operations into the mix

00:07:52.851 --> 00:07:56.180
with main memory
operations, guess what?

00:07:56.181 --> 00:07:58.385
The performance declines.

00:07:58.386 --> 00:08:01.170
What I've done here with
these lines is simply show

00:08:01.171 --> 00:08:04.825
that this is a weighted average
of the operations,

00:08:04.826 --> 00:08:06.370
and we did some experiments,

00:08:06.371 --> 00:08:07.755
actually James did them,

00:08:07.756 --> 00:08:12.270
which produced the results
in the dots in between,

00:08:12.271 --> 00:08:16.700
and I summarized the
results as the ratio

00:08:16.701 --> 00:08:18.740
between secondary
storage operations and

00:08:18.741 --> 00:08:21.990
main memory operations
is around 5.8.

00:08:22.300 --> 00:08:25.205
So, that says that

00:08:25.206 --> 00:08:28.380
if you have memory on
secondary storage,

00:08:28.381 --> 00:08:31.640
you can expect that
your performance will not be as

00:08:31.641 --> 00:08:36.345
good as it will be if the data
is always in main memory.

00:08:36.346 --> 00:08:37.830
&gt;&gt; So, what is

00:08:37.831 --> 00:08:41.460
performance measured
as? Is that throughput?

00:08:41.461 --> 00:08:43.080
&gt;&gt; Yeah. Exactly.

00:08:43.081 --> 00:08:44.415
&gt;&gt; So it's relative throughput?

00:08:44.416 --> 00:08:46.120
&gt;&gt; Yes that's right.

00:08:48.360 --> 00:08:50.860
So, this looks like it

00:08:50.861 --> 00:08:52.569
might be a performance disaster,

00:08:52.570 --> 00:08:57.300
say each additional secondary
storage operation

00:08:57.301 --> 00:08:59.780
pushes the performance lower,

00:08:59.781 --> 00:09:02.729
and it pushes it
actually toward whatever

00:09:02.730 --> 00:09:05.805
the secondary storage
operations are.

00:09:05.806 --> 00:09:08.985
So, when you're at the,
let me go back a minute,

00:09:08.986 --> 00:09:10.620
when you're at this end of

00:09:10.621 --> 00:09:12.820
the graph you're getting
main memory performance,

00:09:12.821 --> 00:09:14.935
at least for the
caching system main memory,

00:09:14.936 --> 00:09:16.990
and at the far end you're
getting something close

00:09:16.991 --> 00:09:19.795
to secondary storage operations.

00:09:19.796 --> 00:09:21.685
&gt;&gt; Secondary storage,
you're assuming is,

00:09:21.686 --> 00:09:22.870
so I don't mistake this.

00:09:22.871 --> 00:09:25.070
&gt;&gt; I'm assuming it's flash.

00:09:26.880 --> 00:09:29.740
So, why bother?

00:09:29.741 --> 00:09:31.970
Why bother with caching systems?

00:09:31.971 --> 00:09:34.075
Because costs matter.

00:09:34.076 --> 00:09:36.920
So, now I want to take a look at,

00:09:36.921 --> 00:09:38.110
and let's go to
the heart of the talk,

00:09:38.111 --> 00:09:39.850
let's look at
cost performance not

00:09:39.851 --> 00:09:42.575
just performance but
cost-performance.

00:09:42.576 --> 00:09:46.105
There are two costs
for operating on data,

00:09:46.106 --> 00:09:48.050
there are storage costs.

00:09:48.051 --> 00:09:49.775
Storage costs are paid off

00:09:49.776 --> 00:09:53.025
all the time data has
to sit some place.

00:09:53.026 --> 00:09:57.070
Storage costs are most of
the cost for cold data.

00:09:57.071 --> 00:09:59.070
So, what is that? Why is that?

00:09:59.071 --> 00:10:00.390
Well, I hope you're
not operating on it,

00:10:00.391 --> 00:10:01.855
so there's no execution cost.

00:10:01.856 --> 00:10:04.590
So, storage cost is a factor.

00:10:04.591 --> 00:10:06.230
Whenever you have a big factor,

00:10:06.231 --> 00:10:08.895
whenever you have
relatively cold data.

00:10:08.896 --> 00:10:11.350
The other cost is execution cost.

00:10:11.351 --> 00:10:12.860
This cost is pay when you're

00:10:12.861 --> 00:10:15.320
actually executing on the data,

00:10:15.321 --> 00:10:18.385
and it's most of the cost of

00:10:18.386 --> 00:10:21.980
a hot data or when
data becomes hot.

00:10:21.981 --> 00:10:27.960
So, if you're really
banging on data very hard,

00:10:27.961 --> 00:10:30.460
you're going to be paying
the execution cost

00:10:30.461 --> 00:10:31.520
and this is where the main memory

00:10:31.521 --> 00:10:33.100
systems have their advantages,

00:10:33.101 --> 00:10:38.685
but if you're looking to
serve as cold data as well,

00:10:38.686 --> 00:10:40.225
the cold data is
going to be sitting

00:10:40.226 --> 00:10:41.890
on secondary storage if you have

00:10:41.891 --> 00:10:43.490
a caching system
whereas if you have

00:10:43.491 --> 00:10:44.590
a main memory system it's going

00:10:44.591 --> 00:10:45.960
to be sitting in main memory,

00:10:45.961 --> 00:10:48.265
and main memory
systems as you'll see

00:10:48.266 --> 00:10:52.395
are more costly than
secondary storage.

00:10:52.396 --> 00:10:55.100
So, it's about
relative costs here.

00:10:55.101 --> 00:10:58.240
So, we have storage costs
and we have execution costs.

00:10:58.241 --> 00:11:01.650
So, if you look at,
these are the ratios

00:11:01.651 --> 00:11:05.530
between main memory cost
and flash storage.

00:11:05.531 --> 00:11:07.730
There aren't any numbers here.

00:11:07.731 --> 00:11:09.560
There were numbers
in my Daymond paper,

00:11:09.561 --> 00:11:10.900
but there aren't any
numbers here, this is

00:11:10.901 --> 00:11:13.060
just showing relative clause.

00:11:13.061 --> 00:11:15.470
So, main memory is
approximately a factor of

00:11:15.471 --> 00:11:18.420
10 more expensive than
flash memory.

00:11:18.421 --> 00:11:22.255
On the other side when you
talk about execution cost,

00:11:22.256 --> 00:11:26.680
these are numbers which are
derived from the BW tree.

00:11:26.681 --> 00:11:32.605
The main memory operation is
very low cost and the flash,

00:11:32.606 --> 00:11:36.425
executing stuff from
flash memory is very costly.

00:11:36.426 --> 00:11:38.745
Let me come over here and
talk to you about this.

00:11:38.746 --> 00:11:41.380
So, here's, after you bring

00:11:41.381 --> 00:11:43.880
stuff in from flash you
have to execute on it,

00:11:43.881 --> 00:11:45.430
so that's this cost here.

00:11:45.431 --> 00:11:47.525
This is the cost of the IO path.

00:11:47.526 --> 00:11:51.580
This is the cost of the processor

00:11:51.581 --> 00:11:55.945
executing the IO instruction
going through the IO stack,

00:11:55.946 --> 00:11:57.995
and this is the cost of buying

00:11:57.996 --> 00:12:01.805
a secondary storage
operation on flash.

00:12:01.806 --> 00:12:04.520
So, these operations on flash,

00:12:04.521 --> 00:12:06.260
you have to pay for them too.

00:12:06.261 --> 00:12:07.720
This is analogous to what

00:12:07.721 --> 00:12:09.380
Ray talked about
when he talked about

00:12:09.381 --> 00:12:15.560
mine disk operations in
his five minute wallpaper. Yeah.

00:12:15.750 --> 00:12:17.500
&gt;&gt; Is this post analysis?
Are you considering

00:12:17.501 --> 00:12:19.115
also the data volume?

00:12:19.116 --> 00:12:22.525
For example, if you have
five terabytes of data,

00:12:22.526 --> 00:12:24.210
and in memory system.

00:12:24.211 --> 00:12:25.570
Buying a five terabyte in

00:12:25.571 --> 00:12:27.560
memory system is going
to be inordinately more

00:12:27.561 --> 00:12:29.080
expensive than a 100 gigabyte in

00:12:29.081 --> 00:12:31.095
memory system per
gigabyte, right?

00:12:31.096 --> 00:12:32.960
Whereas you can easily have

00:12:32.961 --> 00:12:35.470
a five terabyte MVM system

00:12:35.471 --> 00:12:38.050
which is more or less
commodities this days.

00:12:38.051 --> 00:12:39.200
So, I think the price

00:12:39.201 --> 00:12:42.205
also probably starts
increasing as soon,

00:12:42.206 --> 00:12:44.905
if you want go with
larger data volumes.

00:12:44.906 --> 00:12:46.590
&gt;&gt; There's any number of

00:12:46.591 --> 00:12:48.450
ways of making this more complex.

00:12:48.451 --> 00:12:51.000
&gt;&gt; Okay. So, assuming

00:12:51.001 --> 00:12:53.320
the standard like
maybe 100 gigabytes

00:12:53.321 --> 00:12:54.370
or that kind of data type.

00:12:54.371 --> 00:12:59.830
&gt;&gt; This is sort of a standard
commodity prices out there.

00:12:59.831 --> 00:13:00.100
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:13:00.101 --> 00:13:04.550
&gt;&gt; So, either the relative costs

00:13:04.551 --> 00:13:08.975
and I'll have some more things
to say about that later,

00:13:08.976 --> 00:13:10.975
and the reason why,

00:13:10.976 --> 00:13:14.950
so previously it used to cost
you a lot to buy an IO axis

00:13:14.951 --> 00:13:17.160
but the current
assets deeds give you

00:13:17.161 --> 00:13:20.140
hundreds of thousands of IOPS,

00:13:20.141 --> 00:13:23.590
and so the end result is
that that drives down

00:13:23.591 --> 00:13:27.690
the cost of buying
IOPS substantially.

