00:34:04.461 --> 00:34:09.330
does is we support BlindWrite.

00:34:09.331 --> 00:34:12.280
What is a BlindWrite?
A BlindWrite is I'm going

00:34:12.281 --> 00:34:17.890
to update sometimes called
the virus into an upsert.

00:34:17.891 --> 00:34:20.730
Doesn't matter what
the state of the object

00:34:20.731 --> 00:34:23.570
was before, it wasn't objects.

00:34:23.571 --> 00:34:26.155
We're going to create
something and put it in

00:34:26.156 --> 00:34:27.920
the new state of
the object regardless

00:34:27.921 --> 00:34:30.115
of what the old state was.

00:34:30.116 --> 00:34:31.810
So, you don't have to look at

00:34:31.811 --> 00:34:35.820
the old state to
perform this operation.

00:34:35.821 --> 00:34:38.055
If you don't require that the

00:34:38.056 --> 00:34:42.079
operation changes data
in place someplace.

00:34:42.080 --> 00:34:45.000
So, in RocksDB world,

00:34:45.001 --> 00:34:47.905
they have a local in-memory
table which they'll update.

00:34:47.906 --> 00:34:51.050
For us, we do an update by

00:34:51.051 --> 00:34:54.340
posting a delta update to
our mapping table in memory.

00:34:54.341 --> 00:34:56.270
So, a BlindWrite don't

00:34:56.271 --> 00:35:00.010
require access to get

00:35:00.011 --> 00:35:03.240
