So, performance at baseline
set of point experiments

00:40:05.671 --> 00:40:07.305
executed on our machines

00:40:07.306 --> 00:40:09.540
using mostly are
Deuteronomy system.

00:40:09.541 --> 00:40:14.070
So, you generalize it

00:40:14.071 --> 00:40:15.985
at some risk but

00:40:15.986 --> 00:40:19.235
I think they tell a story
that's interesting.

00:40:19.236 --> 00:40:21.790
The costs are taken from the web.

00:40:21.791 --> 00:40:23.960
I scoured the web
looking for cost.

00:40:23.961 --> 00:40:25.400
They vary a lot even in

00:40:25.401 --> 00:40:27.700
one instant of time and are
changing over time, right?

00:40:27.701 --> 00:40:33.600
So, it's not something
which is etched in stone

00:40:33.601 --> 00:40:37.410
here like that your

00:40:37.411 --> 00:40:39.910
next comment on
the Amazon gravestone.

00:40:39.911 --> 00:40:41.995
It's not etched in stone.

00:40:41.996 --> 00:40:44.150
But, I think that
the general thrust of

00:40:44.151 --> 00:40:47.180
results gives you a good way
of thinking about

00:40:47.181 --> 00:40:49.500
how to design your system and why

00:40:49.501 --> 00:40:53.340
data caching systems are
in fact a very good idea.

00:40:53.880 --> 00:40:56.650
So, that's one caveat.

00:40:56.651 --> 00:40:59.290
Second caveat is
best cost performance is

00:40:59.291 --> 00:41:02.145
not the only thing that
you need to worry about.

00:41:02.146 --> 00:41:04.260
Okay. Maybe it may not

00:41:04.261 --> 00:41:07.925
even be the primary thing
that you worry about.

00:41:07.926 --> 00:41:09.445
So certainly, it's always

00:41:09.446 --> 00:41:11.890
the case that low cost is a plus.

00:41:11.891 --> 00:41:17.290
But, what you want to maximize
is not minimizing cost.

00:41:17.291 --> 00:41:19.060
You want to maximize value

00:41:19.061 --> 00:41:21.170
which is the difference
between what

00:41:21.171 --> 00:41:23.210
you earn from processing

00:41:23.211 --> 00:41:25.395
the data and what
it cost you, right?

00:41:25.396 --> 00:41:29.290
So, if you've one of
these applications which has

00:41:29.291 --> 00:41:34.505
really high value tremendous
needs for low latency,

00:41:34.506 --> 00:41:36.925
maybe you don't want to
use a data caching system.

00:41:36.926 --> 00:41:38.180
Maybe that's the niche where

00:41:38.181 --> 00:41:40.910
a main memory stuff
makes sense, right?

00:41:41.220 --> 00:41:44.095
Because they've got
very high cost,

00:41:44.096 --> 00:41:46.280
but if the value is

00:41:46.281 --> 00:41:49.270
enormous and the value
comes from your being,

00:41:49.271 --> 00:41:51.740
just a little bit faster
than the other guy,

00:41:51.741 --> 00:41:54.480
then you may be willing to spend

00:41:54.481 --> 00:41:55.650
a whole lot more money and not

00:41:55.651 --> 00:41:58.110
worry so much about
the lowest cost.

00:42:01.740 --> 00:42:05.620
So, somebody stock trading things

00:42:05.621 --> 00:42:08.580
are of that flavor but
it's not a whole lot.

00:42:08.581 --> 00:42:10.430
I think the vast majority

00:42:10.431 --> 00:42:12.445
of the applications
are more concerned

00:42:12.446 --> 00:42:15.750
with some sort of thing which

00:42:15.751 --> 00:42:20.640
is suitable for
an interactive system.

00:42:20.641 --> 00:42:23.540
Now, there's
an enormous spectrum not

00:42:23.541 --> 00:42:24.860
trying to say that
there's anything

00:42:24.861 --> 00:42:26.285
in between because there is,

00:42:26.286 --> 00:42:29.410
but you have to
decide which place

00:42:29.411 --> 00:42:31.265
your system fits in that.

00:42:31.266 --> 00:42:35.900
Generally speaking, trying
to minimize your cost,

00:42:35.901 --> 00:42:37.994
maximize your cost performance

00:42:37.995 --> 00:42:40.490
is a goal that most people,

00:42:40.491 --> 00:42:43.150
and certainly the
traditional systems,

00:42:43.151 --> 00:42:46.710
should be aiming at as a way
to keep the majority of

00:42:46.711 --> 00:42:52.410
their customers happy. All right.

00:42:52.640 --> 00:42:57.450
So recap, this is
the critical thing.

00:42:57.451 --> 00:42:58.640
I just want to remind you.

00:42:58.641 --> 00:43:02.415
There's two costs, storage
cost and execution cost.

00:43:02.416 --> 00:43:04.540
You always pay the storage cost,

00:43:04.541 --> 00:43:06.915
even if nothing's
happening on your data.

00:43:06.916 --> 00:43:10.220
So that's a really
significant aspect of that.

00:43:10.221 --> 00:43:12.020
So, if nothing is
happening on your data,

00:43:12.021 --> 00:43:13.860
you want to work as hard as you

00:43:13.861 --> 00:43:16.890
can to reduce the storage costs.

00:43:17.000 --> 00:43:20.680
So, when data is cold,

00:43:20.681 --> 00:43:24.105
storage cost is most of the cost.

00:43:24.106 --> 00:43:26.050
When data is hot, then you say,

00:43:26.051 --> 00:43:28.420
well, okay execution cost
really starts to matter here.

00:43:28.421 --> 00:43:33.280
But most data, most
of the time is cold.

00:43:33.281 --> 00:43:40.165
They win by moving data
between the low-cost medium,

00:43:40.166 --> 00:43:43.539
which doesn't have
as much performance

00:43:43.540 --> 00:43:45.675
but has a higher cost
of operation.

00:43:45.676 --> 00:43:47.270
They move it from there to

00:43:47.271 --> 00:43:49.600
the high-cost medium,
main memory,

00:43:49.601 --> 00:43:53.410
which is higher cost on a sort

00:43:53.411 --> 00:43:54.590
of a time basis for

00:43:54.591 --> 00:43:57.610
the storage but it has
a lower execution cost,

00:43:57.611 --> 00:44:00.065
and you switch between the two

00:44:00.066 --> 00:44:04.105
based on what you approximate
to be the crossover point.

00:44:04.106 --> 00:44:07.050
So, the moral is this,

00:44:07.051 --> 00:44:08.100
we should be focusing on

00:44:08.101 --> 00:44:11.160
data caching systems
and their improvements.

